{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787727972,"nonce":"bUCGrp1LNZmonaSUclFuR915Yf8=","title":"Cache restores at Depot replicaCount\u003e1: warmth-affinity routing OR durable cache copies","message":"ADR-0065 s1 (dbe05e5) shipped the keyed directory Cache WARM-ONLY, and warm is replica-local. The honest arithmetic at N\u003e1: a saved cache tree lives whole on the ONE replica that took the drain, but a restore's blob/tree GETs scatter independently across replicas (no affinity on the fetch path), so for a real dir of f files the probability every GET lands on the holder is ~(1/N)^f — effectively ZERO for any real node_modules/target. Every such restore degrades to the tolerated miss: correct by construction (miss-is-slower-never-wrong is asserted), but the hit rate silently collapses the day an operator scales the Depot.\n\nTwo candidate fixes, to be priced against each other with the instrumentation dbe05e5 built (cache_entries.saved_at refresh + fetcher hit/miss log lines):\n\n1. Warmth-affinity restore routing: route a cache restore's reads to the replica that recorded the save (the cache_entries row could carry a replica hint, or the fetcher could ask /have per replica first). Cheap, keeps warm-only semantics; adds a routing surface.\n2. Durable cache copies: let a cache save opt into the pack path (consuming pack rows + object storage for evictable content) — exactly the trade ADR-0065's evidence bar describes; reopen ONLY if measured warm lifetime \u003c key lifetime shows caches dying before reuse.\n\nRelated: cba7165 (no warm eviction) — eviction pressure at N\u003e1 makes both the miss rate and this decision more urgent. Also note ADR-0066: fence affinity is already a correctness requirement for drains at N\u003e1; a cache-restore affinity mechanism should ride whatever solves that, not invent a second scheme.","files":null}]}